# ------------------------------------------------------- build artefacts
# Everything `make clean` removes, plus the shapes a debug or instrumented
# build adds. The Makefile builds in the source directory rather than a build/
# subdirectory, so every object lands beside the source it came from and the
# patterns have to be shape-based rather than path-based.
*.o
/saber
/saberctl
/vinput

# Libraries. Saber builds executables only, so these are a standing guard
# rather than a rule for anything the tree produces today.
*.a
*.so
*.so.*

# Coverage and sanitizer output. `make DEBUG=YES ASAN=YES` is a documented
# build, and an instrumented run drops these beside the binary.
*.gcno
*.gcda
*.gcov
lcov.info
/coverage/

# Compiler intermediates from -save-temps, used when reading what the optimiser
# did. Never an input.
*.i
*.ii

# Generated by wayland-scanner. Never committed -- regenerated by `make` from
# protocol/*.xml and from ${WAYLAND_PROTOCOLS}.
*-protocol.h
*-protocol.c
version.h

# The release tarball built by `make dist`.
saber-*.tar.gz

# NOTE: share/man/man1/saber.1 is deliberately NOT ignored. Nothing generates
# it -- the Makefile has no man target and `install:` does not install one -- so
# it is a hand-written artifact that ships, referenced by README.md, and a rule
# matching it here would silently swallow it the moment it is written.

# Core dumps
*.core

# Agent process docs: machine-specific development notes, not product code.
# Kept on disk for the AGENTS.md workflow, deliberately not published --
# the same treatment hikari-sakura gives its own .devdocs/.
.devdocs/

# NOTE: ports/ is deliberately NOT ignored. The FreeBSD port skeleton was held
# back until submission on the grounds that a MAINTAINER line is a public
# commitment to a reachable address; that call was reversed on 2026-09-09, and
# it now ships. README.md links to it, so an ignore rule here breaks that link
# rather than protecting anything. It is a skeleton, not a submitted port --
# see ports/Makefile and README.md, both of which say so.

.claude/

# Captured runtime logs from redirect typos (e.g. `2>1` instead of `2>&1`)
/1
/2

# --------------------------------------------------------------- secrets
# Nothing here is used by Saber today. These are standing guards: a key or a
# credential dropped into the tree during debugging must never become a
# commit, and a rule that only appears after the mistake is a rule too late.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.keystore
*.jks
id_rsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519
*.gpg
*.asc
secrets.*
credentials.*
.netrc
.npmrc
.pypirc
.aws/
.ssh/

# ------------------------------------------------- local / machine-specific
# Editor and tool state, personal overrides, and anything scoped to one host.
# Same reasoning as .devdocs/ above: development context, not product.
# (.claude/ is already covered above.)
.cursor/
.aider*
.vscode/
.idea/
.zed/
.helix/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.local
*.local.*
compile_commands.json
.cache/

# Code-navigation indexes. Regenerated from the source in seconds and large
# enough to dominate a diff. `.clangd` and `compile_flags.txt` ARE tracked --
# they are the checked-in configuration those tools read, not their output.
tags
TAGS
.tags
cscope.*
.ccls-cache/

# Per-developer shell environment. Machine-specific by definition, and the
# usual place an exported credential ends up.
.envrc
.direnv/

# Ad-hoc scratch: crash traces, session captures, test scribbles.
*.log
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej
*.tmp
/scratch/
/tmp/
core
core.*
vgcore.*
